  "worked_solution": "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $4\\,kg$ at a rate of $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=4(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})=(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061e+{e}^{4}-(4\\operatorname{ln}\u20611+{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =4+{e}^{4}-0-1 \\\\  & =({e}^{4}+3)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
